# Collector environment example
#
# Copy what you need into the private collector.env created by the installer
# (mode 0600). The generated file starts with every value commented out: with
# no destinations the collector fails closed and delivers nothing.
#
# PUBLIC DEMO VALUES ONLY. demo-token-local is a deliberately public local test
# string, not a credential for any real deployment.

# COLLECTOR_MODE=real
# Unacknowledged samples wait in RAM by default, so routine delivery writes no
# queue to disk and a collector restart loses what was never acknowledged.
# Set this only when surviving a collector restart with unacknowledged samples
# is worth one database write per sample; it is bounded per destination to
# 10 minutes and 64 MiB.
# COLLECTOR_QUEUE_MODE=durable
# COLLECTOR_STATE_FILE=/home/operator/.local/state/rigscope/collector/collector.json
# COLLECTOR_INTERVAL_MS=1000
# COLLECTOR_HOSTNAME=rig-01

# Enroll once against the hub, then remove the one-use grant from the hub.
# COLLECTOR_ENROLL_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:8081/api/collector/connect
# COLLECTOR_ENROLL_TOKEN=<one-use grant from the hub>

# Or supply destinations explicitly (enrollment writes these into private state
# for real installations). Full websocket URL including the connect path:
# COLLECTOR_HUBS=[{"url":"ws://127.0.0.1:8081/api/collector/connect","token":"demo-token-local"}]

# Per-destination sharing policy can withhold sensitive families. Defaults are
# CPU/memory only; process details remain request-scoped and are never spooled.
